## Fixing a Bug: AVIF Images Saved as .jpg That Wouldn't Open on Windows

### Background
A user reported that images downloaded from our service would show an error on Windows - "The file format is unsupported or the file is corrupted." The same files opened fine on Mac or in Chrome, which made the root cause hard to pinpoint initially.

![Windows Photos showing an "unsupported format" error](/images/answers/windows-open-error.svg)

### Root Cause
Running a file format check in the terminal revealed that some `.jpg` files were actually **AVIF** under the hood.

![Terminal `file` output - test.jpg is AVIF, umsungjun.jpg is JPEG](/images/answers/avif-file-check.svg)

- `test.jpg` → ISO Media, **AVIF Image** (extension mismatch with actual format)
- `umsungjun.jpg` → JPEG image data (normal)

It turned out that Android phones internally save screenshots in AVIF format while still appending a `.jpg` extension. AVIF is a modern, high-efficiency codec with limited support on older Windows environments, which caused the failures.

### Solution
I checked the Blob's `type` field (the actual MIME type, not the filename extension) after fetching the file. If the MIME type was `image/avif`, I used the **Canvas API** to re-encode the image as JPEG before triggering the download.

```typescript
const convertIfAvif = async (blob: Blob): Promise<Blob> => {
  if (blob.type === 'image/avif') {
    const img = await createImageBitmap(blob);
    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
    canvas.width = img.width;
    canvas.height = img.height;
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    if (!ctx) throw new Error('Canvas context creation failed');

    ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
    return new Promise<Blob>((resolve, reject) => {
      canvas.toBlob(
        (newBlob) => {
          if (newBlob) resolve(newBlob);
          else reject(new Error('JPG conversion failed'));
        },
        'image/jpeg',
        0.9
      );
    });
  }
  return blob;
};
```

Two additional details mattered: **forcing the file extension to `.jpg`** after conversion, and calling `revokeObjectURL` afterward to prevent memory leaks.

### Outcome
Downloads now work correctly on Windows. The key takeaway: **the file extension is just a label - the actual MIME type is what determines how the OS handles the file.** Since then, I've made it a habit to validate MIME types explicitly whenever writing file-handling logic.

